Instruction: Read the segment of a merger agreement and answer the multiple-choice question by choosing the option that best characterizes the agreement.\nQuestion: Is the \u201cability to consummate\u201d concept subject to Material Adverse Effect (MAE) carveouts?\nOption A: No\nOption B: Yes\n\nMerger Agreement: \u201cCompany Material Adverse Effect\u201d means any change, event, violation, inaccuracy, effect or circumstance (each, an \u201cEffect\u201d) that, individually or taken together with all other Effects, (a) has had or would reasonably be expected to have a material adverse effect on the business, assets, liabilities, financial condition or results of operations of the Company and its Subsidiaries, taken as a whole, or (b) would, or would reasonably be expected to, prevent, materially delay or materially impair the ability of the Company to consummate the transactions contemplated by this Agreement; provided that, in the case of clause (a) only, none of the following (by itself or when aggregated) will be deemed to be or constitute a Company Material Adverse Effect: (i) changes in general economic conditions; (ii) changes in conditions in the financial markets; (iii) changes in regulatory, legislative or political conditions; (iv) acts of war, sabotage, terrorism or military actions; (v) natural disasters or pandemics, including COVID-19; (vi) any action taken or refrained from being taken which Parent has expressly approved in writing. However, with respect to clause (b), the following will not be considered as preventing, materially delaying, or materially impairing the ability to consummate the transactions: (vii) the public announcement or pendency of this Agreement; (viii) any changes in the price or trading volume of the Company Common Stock; (ix) any failure to meet financial projections, unless such failure is caused by a factor not excluded under this definition. (Pages 12-14)\nAnswer:
Choices:

